Project Title:Predicting Daily Maximum Temperature and Humidity at Manchester 2050–2080Course:Environmental Monitoring and Modelling — Group Project1. Project Overview and Work SummaryThis project focused on evaluating and predicting future climate risks, specifically the daily maximum near-surface air temperature (TREFMXAV_U) and specific humidity (QBOT) at Manchester for the period of 2050–2080.The analysis was built upon the Community Earth System Model 2, Large Ensemble (CESM2 LENS2).My team and I processed a massive dataset consisting of 164,244 total daily observations across 6 ensemble members (003–008), extracted from the nearest grid point to Manchester.My primary responsibility involved conducting Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) to uncover temporal trends, engineering features for supervised machine learning models, and assessing compound hot-humid climate risks.To ensure our work was rigorous and reproducible, we utilized GitHub for version control, provided anenvironment.ymlfor conda environment recreation (Python 3.12), and fixed random seeds to 42 for all models.2. In-Depth Data Analysis and Key FindingsThrough extensive EDA, we identified several critical patterns regarding Manchester's future climate shift:Significant Warming Trend:We observed a statistically significant linear upward trend in annual mean temperature, recording a warming rate of +0.3°C per decade.The ensemble mean is projected to rise from roughly 10°C to 12°C over the 2006–2080 period, marking a total warming of +2.2°CUneven Seasonal Shifts:The warming pattern is not uniform; summer months will warm significantly more than winter months.We found the largest shift occurs in August at +2.4°C, while the smallest shift is in December at +1.1°C.Moistening and Humidity Trends:We also extended our framework to specific humidity (QBOT), revealing a clear moistening trend of +0.152 g/kg per decade.The future mean QBOT is projected to rise from 6.30 g/kg to 6.89 g/kg, with July and August showing the strongest moistening.Escalating Compound Risks:A major finding was the strong positive relationship between daily maximum temperature and humidity (Pearson r = 0.791).By setting thresholds at the historical 90th percentile (P90), we identified a clear increase in compound hot-humid days after 2050, indicating a rising risk of simultaneous heat and humidity extremes.3. Machine Learning Methodology and PerformanceTo predict future values, we employed a strict temporal split, training the models on historical data (2006–2049, 
generating 96,354 samples) and testing on future projections (2050–2080, generating 67,890 samples).Feature Engineering:We utilized 11 features in total.This included 8 climate variables directly from the data (such asTREFHT,FSNS,FLNS) and engineered temporal features likesin(doy),cos(doy), and a normalized year.Importantly,TREFMXAV_Uwas deliberately excluded as a feature to force the model to learn underlying climate dynamics.Model Comparison:We tested three models: Ridge Regression (as a linear baseline), Random Forest (to capture non-linear patterns), and Gradient Boosting (for sequential error correction).Results:All three models explained over 97% of the variance (R² > 0.97).The Random Forest performed best overall for temperature, achieving the lowest Mean Absolute Error (0.51°C) and the highest R² (0.984).Feature importance analysis confirmed that mean temperature (TREFHT) dominated predictive power, though solar radiation (FSNS) also contributed meaningfully.Additionally, ablation tests for humidity models showed that removing bothTmaxandTREFHTstrongly increased the Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) and dropped the R² clearly.4. Reflections on Shortcomings and LimitationsWhile the models successfully predicted future dynamics, reflecting on the project reveals several structural and methodological shortcomings that limit the robustness of our conclusionsOver-reliance on a Single Data Source:The entire analysis is fundamentally constrained because the results are based on only one climate-model framework (CESM2 LENS2). This makes our findings vulnerable to the specific biases inherent in this single model.Lack of Spatial Diversity:The study focuses exclusively on one specific Manchester grid point. By not incorporating surrounding regional data, the analysis fails to capture potential micro-climatic variations or broader geographic trends.Methodological Simplicity:The machine learning framework we applied is relatively simple and primarily demonstrates predictive relationships rather than explaining complex physical climate mechanisms.Furthermore, as our EDA showed, summer humidity was the hardest variable to predict (showing the highest RMSE), indicating that our current feature set may not fully capture the volatile nature of warm-season moisture.5. Directions for Future ImprovementTo address the aforementioned limitations and elevate the academic rigor of my future data analysis work, I have formulated the following actionable recommendations:Enhance Data Robustness:I will compare projections across multiple climate models and integrate real-world observational datasets to cross-validate findings and reduce model-specific uncertainties.Broaden the Analytical Scope:I plan to extend the analysis to encompass more geographic locations, allowing for a comparative spatial study rather than an isolated single-point analysis.
Deepen the Modeling Approach:Moving forward, I will utilize stricter validation techniques.Additionally, to better address the compound hot-humid risks we identified, I will incorporate more advanced and standardized heat-stress indicators to fully contextualize the physiological impacts of future climate shifts.
